---
import Compare from "../components/Compare.astro";
---

<section id="unions">
  <h2>26. Unions, intersections, discriminated unions</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// C# has no union types — hierarchy + pattern matching
public abstract record Result;
public record Ok(string Value) : Result;
public record Err(string Message) : Result;

string Show(Result r) =&gt; r switch
&#123;
  Ok o =&gt; $"ok: &#123;o.Value&#125;",
  Err e =&gt; $"err: &#123;e.Message&#125;",
  _ =&gt; throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException()
&#125;;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>type Result =
  | &#123; ok: true; value: string &#125;
  | &#123; ok: false; message: string &#125;;

function show(r: Result): string &#123;
  return r.ok ? `ok: $&#123;r.value&#125;` : `err: $&#123;r.message&#125;`;
&#125;

// intersection = "both of"
type Named = &#123; name: string &#125;;
type Aged = &#123; age: number &#125;;
type Person = Named &amp; Aged;

// keyof / typeof / indexed access — no C# equivalent:
type Keys = keyof Person; // "name" | "age"</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>Biggest modeling gap. In TS reach for unions of literals; in C# reach for <code>abstract record</code> hierarchies.</li>
    <li>TS <code>|</code> ≈ C# inheritance for “one of”; TS <code>&amp;</code> ≈ multiple interfaces for “both of”.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr />

<section id="immutability">
  <h2>27. Immutability &amp; readonly — in depth</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>public record Person(string Name, int Age);
var a = new Person("Ada", 36);
var b = a with &#123; Age = 37 &#125;; // non-destructive

IReadOnlyList&lt;int&gt; nums = new List&lt;int&gt; &#123; 1, 2 &#125;;
// nums.Add → not available

public class Cfg &#123; public string Env &#123; get; init; &#125; = "dev"; &#125;
// init: set once at creation</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>type Person = &#123; readonly name: string; readonly age: number &#125;;
const a: Person = &#123; name: "Ada", age: 36 &#125;;
const b: Person = &#123; ...a, age: 37 &#125;;

const nums: readonly number[] = [1, 2];
// nums.push → error
type Frozen&lt;T&gt; = &#123; readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K] &#125;;
type DeepRO&lt;T&gt; = &#123; readonly [K in keyof T]: DeepRO&lt;T[K]&gt; &#125;;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>Both favor copy-on-write: C# <code>with</code>, TS object/array spread.</li>
    <li>TS <code>readonly</code> is shallow + compile-time only — use <code>Object.freeze</code> for runtime.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr />

<section id="attributes">
  <h2>28. Attributes, decorators, reflection</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>[Obsolete("Use NewMethod instead")]
public void OldMethod() &#123; /* ... */ &#125;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class)]
public class RouteAttribute(string path) : Attribute
&#123; public string Path =&gt; path; &#125;

[Route("/users")]
public class UsersController &#123; &#125;

var attr = typeof(UsersController)
  .GetCustomAttributes(typeof(RouteAttribute), false)
  .Cast&lt;RouteAttribute&gt;().First();

// source generators: compile-time codegen, AOT-safe</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>/** @deprecated Use newMethod instead. */
export function oldMethod() &#123;&#125;

// TC39 decorators (TS 5+)
function Route(path: string) &#123;
  return function (target: Function) &#123;
    Reflect.defineMetadata?.("route", path, target);
  &#125;;
&#125;
@Route("/users")
export class UsersController &#123; &#125;

// reflection ≈ manual metadata + schemas
// runtime type info is NOT automatic like C#
// prefer Zod/Valibot over decorator magic for validation</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>C# attributes + reflection are runtime-first (ASP.NET, serializers). TS decorators are syntax sugar — runtime types need explicit libraries.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr />

<section id="equality">
  <h2>29. Equality &amp; reference vs value</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>string a = "hi"; string b = "hi";
bool eq = a == b; // true (string value equality)

var p1 = new Person("Ada", 36); // record
var p2 = new Person("Ada", 36);
bool sameRecord = p1 == p2; // true (value)

var o1 = new object(); var o2 = new object();
bool sameRef = ReferenceEquals(o1, o2); // false
bool eqDef = EqualityComparer&lt;int&gt;.Default.Equals(1, 1);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>const a = "hi"; const b = "hi";
const eq = a === b; // true (primitives by value)

const p1 = &#123; name: "Ada" &#125;; const p2 = &#123; name: "Ada" &#125;;
const sameObj = p1 === p2; // false! (reference)
// deep: isDeepStrictEqual, lodash.isEqual, fast-deep-equal
// == does coercion: 0 == "" is true — always use === / !==

// NaN gotcha: NaN !== NaN → use Number.isNaN</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>C# <code>==</code> can be overloaded per type. JS/TS <code>===</code> is never overloaded: primitives by value, objects by reference.</li>
    <li>Always <code>===/!==</code> in TS. In C# follow each type’s semantics (<code>record</code> = value, <code>class</code> = reference unless overridden).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Memory model footnote</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// struct = stack/value (usually), class = heap/reference
// Span&lt;T&gt; / stackalloc for zero-alloc slices
Span&lt;int&gt; s = stackalloc int[3] &#123; 1, 2, 3 &#125;;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>// everything is heap + GC; primitives by value
// TypedArrays / ArrayBuffer for zero-copy binary:
const buf = new Uint8Array([1, 2, 3]);
const view = buf.subarray(0, 2); // no copy (like Span)</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
</section>
